Rubella
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Three-day descending maculopapular rash, Tender suboccipital/postauricular lymphadenopathy, Forchheimer spots on soft palate, Mild systemic symptoms

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-046
Reason for Visit: Sudden eruption of a spreading facial rash, low-grade fever, and painful swollen glands in the neck requiring urgent assessment to rule out measles and confirm the diagnosis for public health/pregnancy exposure reasons.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young adult experiencing a rapidly spreading rash starting on the face, accompanied by fever and painful neck swelling. Urgent physical assessment is required to definitively distinguish this presentation from highly toxic measles, manage severe joint pain, and initiate immediate isolation protocols to protect potentially pregnant contacts prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up covered in pink spots starting on my face, and the back of my neck has these incredibly painful, swollen lumps."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 3 days ago with a low-grade fever, mild coryza, and distinct mild conjunctivitis unassociated with severe purulent exudate. Over the last 48 hours, the patient developed prominent, tender, and localized posterior cervical, suboccipital, and postauricular lymphadenopathy. This morning, a distinctive, discrete erythematous maculopapular rash erupted, spreading rapidly from the face downwards to the trunk and limbs. The patient, an adult female, also notes mild, sudden symmetrical polyarthralgia and distinct joint stiffness in her wrists and hands. She reports a complete absence of severe, prostrating systemic toxicity (feels generally well despite the rash). Patient is unsure of her childhood vaccination history.
Functional Status: Ambulatory and independent; mildly uncomfortable due to joint stiffness and tender lymph nodes.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient works as a childcare educator; high risk of exposing pregnant mothers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing despite the florid rash, entirely lacking the toxic, "measles-like" prostration.
Physical Exam: Skin exam reveals a fine, discrete, pinkish maculopapular rash on the face, trunk, and proximal extremities. Head and neck exam reveals exquisitely tender suboccipital and postauricular lymphadenopathy. Oropharyngeal exam reveals Forchheimer spots (distinct pinpoint petechiae specifically on the soft palate). Eye exam shows mild conjunctival injection without purulence. Joints (wrists/hands) have mild restricted range of motion but no effusions. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Three-day descending maculopapular rash, Tender suboccipital/postauricular lymphadenopathy, Forchheimer spots on soft palate, and Mild systemic symptoms.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Not currently pregnant (LMP 2 weeks ago, negative home test today).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Rubella (German Measles) - Status: Stable but highly contagious
Differentials:

Measles (Rubeola) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of severe systemic toxicity, high fever (>40C), severe barking cough, and pathognomonic Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa.

Parvovirus B19 (Fifth Disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rash is discrete and maculopapular rather than the classic "slapped cheek" and lacy reticular rash, though the adult polyarthralgia overlaps significantly.

Drug Eruption - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct viral prodrome, specific postauricular lymphadenopathy, and Forchheimer spots.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Public Health / Teratogenic risk to others)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic, albeit mild, viral exanthem characterized by a descending maculopapular rash, specific postauricular lymphadenopathy, and adult-onset polyarthralgia. While the patient is physically stable, the immediate public health implications of a suspected Rubella case—specifically the devastating teratogenic risk of Congenital Rubella Syndrome to pregnant contacts—mandated an urgent on-site clinical evaluation. An in-person assessment was required to definitively rule out the more immediately life-threatening Measles, confirm the clinical stigmata of Rubella, and instantly enforce strict isolation protocols. This public health emergency strictly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise supportive care (Paracetamol/NSAIDs for joint pain and fever). Mandate strict isolation from all pregnant women and immunocompromised individuals. Do not attend work. Urgent notification to the State Public Health Unit for contact tracing. Request urgent serology (Rubella IgM antibodies and IgG titres) and a viral throat swab for PCR via a home-visit pathology service to prevent clinic exposure.
Positive Behavioral Change: By setting a strict 7-day calendar alert to completely isolate at home, the patient will physically prevent transmission to vulnerable populations; fulfilling this civic duty will reward the patient with the profound relief of knowing they protected unborn children from severe birth defects, reinforcing strict adherence to the quarantine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Public Health Unit follow-up.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 3 days to review serology and assess resolution of the rash.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops a severe, unremitting headache with neck stiffness (rare encephalitis), unusual bleeding or bruising (thrombocytopenia), or severe joint swelling.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically highly suspected; formal serology and PCR are required by Public Health.
Clinical Course: The rash classically vanishes completely within three days. The joint pain, however, may persist for several weeks in adult women before resolving without permanent damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

